WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.057 --> 00:00:01.640
MANU BATRA: Welcome
folks, to KubeCon.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:02.557
My name is Manu Batra.

00:00:02.557 --> 00:00:05.100
I'm the product manager
for Anthos Storage.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.030
JING XU: My name is Jing, so I
am Kubernetes Storage Lifecycle

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.440
Team at Google.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.140
MANU BATRA: Our
session at KubeCon

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.140
was about when you're
coming from a virtual world,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.120
how do you look at
Kubernetes and storage?

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.050
So, I spent 20 years
working on storage,

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.490
but when I moved
into Kubernetes,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.250
I was really looking for a cheat
sheet somebody could walk me

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.260
through to say, how
should I approach storage?

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.580
How should I look at--

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.660
how do I translate A to B?

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.650
So, the talk focuses more
around why does Kubernetes

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:35.580
need storage?

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.160
When do you need storage
for admin clusters

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.500
and also for workflow clusters?

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.440
Then we draw
parallels between when

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.620
you look at architectural
parallels between how

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.320
does working machines approach
storage versus containers

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.182
approach storage?

00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:52.140
So, Jing, do you want to
talk about competitive

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.920
versus declarative models?

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:54.660
JING XU: Sure.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.130
So in the traditional
way of managing systems,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.100
we have imperative APIs.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.100
So, for example, if
you want a new disk,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.780
you perform that task.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.210
If it fails, you try again.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.340
Kubernetes is now
completely changing

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.130
the way you're managing
with declarative APIs.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.820
So, basically, it means you
just describe what the system

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.480
state is and the controller
will automatically reconcile

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.610
until it reaches this state.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.980
If anything goes
wrong, it will try

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.610
to reconcile again until it
reaches this desired state.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.847
So it will attempt
automatically.

00:01:28.847 --> 00:01:29.680
MANU BATRA: Perfect.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.330
So next we move on to
comparing and drawing parallels

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.190
between the operational states.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.340
So, looking at Day 2
operations, about allocations

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.270
of volumes, what are the
different ways containers

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.090
allow you or Kubernetes allows
you to map those volumes?

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.680
And some of the
things we talk about

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.260
is, how do you look at
dynamic provisioning

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.450
versus static provisioning?

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.200
So do you want to talk
about some details?

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.033
JING XU: Yeah, sure.

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:56.720
For static provisioning,
we mean the sysadmin has

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.700
to create, allocate,
partition the disk,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.530
and then the user will use that.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:06.630
But dynamic provisioning means
when the user has a request,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.789
the storage will be
created on demand.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:13.082
So it's done dynamically
instead of preallocated.

00:02:13.082 --> 00:02:15.540
MANU BATRA: And those models
are really critical as you are

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.710
deploying container workloads in
cloud or on-prem because those

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:20.910
models really--

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.350
Day 1 and Day 2 operations
differ greatly on those models.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.490
And in the end,
you really walk out

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.680
with a quick cheat sheet of--

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.755
I'm coming from Planet
A going to Planet B,

00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:33.380
and I'll still be
living on both sides,

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.420
but how do I do that translation
between a terminology

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.023
cheat sheet on what does
A mean in containers,

00:02:39.023 --> 00:02:41.190
and how do I translate to
something that I'm already

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:42.090
familiar with?

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.330
And I can [INAUDIBLE]
much better

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.230
and translate those
things across.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.040
So, thank you for
listening to our talk.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.640
Again, we have a
booth at KubeCon.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.610
Please stop by and feel free
to ask us any questions.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:54.690
We'll be right here.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.723
And welcome to KubeCon.

00:02:56.723 --> 00:02:59.140
JING XU: Yeah, we'll have some
time to talk tomorrow, too.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

